  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Socio-Urban Support of the Integration of Migrant Population</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In recent years, Uruguay has experienced a significant influx of migrants, primarily from Latin American countries, reflecting a broader regional humanitarian and displacement crisis. Migrants arriving in Uruguay face several challenges, including difficulties in accessing the labor market, employment quality, skills mismatches, reconciling work with family responsibilities, housing insecurity, impoverishment, and residential segregation. According to the 2019 Continuous Household Survey, Uruguay hosts 94,000 migrants, with Montevideo having the highest concentration (64.5%). IOM Uruguay&apos;s third DTM Round shows that most migrants reside in Montevideo, particularly in neighborhoods such as Downtown (9%), Barrio Sur (7%), Cord&#xf3;n (7%), Pocitos (5%), and Aguada (5%). A 2020 census by the Municipality of Montevideo found that 40% of residents in boarding houses (pensions) are migrants, mainly located in Municipality B, which includes Ciudad Vieja, Centro, and other nearby areas. Additionally, civil society reports indicate around 200 irregular boarding houses, where 95% of the migrant population live in vulnerable conditions with inadequate living standards. In response to these challenges, IOM, in partnership with the Inter-American Development Bank and the Ministry of Social Development, has coordinated initiatives to support migrant integration in Uruguay. This joint project aims to establish a reception and welcome center that promotes culture, dignity, and access to rights, offering information and integration activities. The center will serve as a hub for institutional services, including regularization, social support, and labor integration, adopting an ethical and humanitarian approach to meet the needs of migrants, asylum seekers, and refugees.</narrative>
  </description>
